The Security Council,

Recalling the Protocol for the Prohibition of the Use in War of Asphyxiating, Poisonous or Other Gases, and of Bacteriological Methods of Warfare, signed at Geneva on 17 June 1925, and the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on their Destruction (Chemical Weapons Convention),

Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2314 (2016), 2319 (2016), and 2401 (2018),

Expressing its grave horror and indignation at the reported use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, in particular the attack in Douma on 7 April 2018, which killed dozens of civilians, including women and children, and injured hundreds more,

Reiterating that the use of chemical weapons constitutes a serious violation of international law, including international humanitarian law, and that those responsible for any use of chemical weapons must be held accountable,

Reaffirming that any use of chemical weapons anywhere, at any time, by anyone, under any circumstances is unacceptable, poses a threat to international peace and security, and is a flagrant violation of international law,

Noting the work of the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) Fact-Finding Mission (FFM) in the Syrian Arab Republic, and welcoming its efforts to deploy immediately to investigate the reported incident in Douma,

Stressing the imperative necessity of establishing an impartial, independent, and professional mechanism to identify individuals, entities, groups, or governments who were perpetrators, organizers, sponsors, or otherwise involved in the use of chemical weapons, including toxic chemicals as weapons, in the Syrian Arab Republic,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the reported use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, in particular the attack on Douma on 7 April 2018, and expresses its deepest condolences to the victims of chemical attacks and their families;

2. Decides to establish the United Nations Independent Mechanism of Investigation (UNIMI) for a period of one year from the date of adoption of this resolution, with a mandate to identify to the greatest extent feasible individuals, entities, groups, or governments who were perpetrators, organizers, sponsors, or otherwise involved in the use of chemical weapons, including chlorine or any other toxic chemical, in the Syrian Arab Republic;

3. Requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the Director-General of the OPCW, to submit to the Security Council, within thirty days of the adoption of this resolution, recommendations, including elements of terms of reference, regarding the establishment and operational functioning of the UNIMI, based on principles of impartiality, independence, and professional expertise;

4. Reaffirms that the Syrian Arab Republic and all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic shall cooperate fully with the OPCW and the UNIMI, including by providing immediate and unfettered access to any and all locations, individuals, materials, and information deemed relevant by the Mechanism, without any preconditions;

5. Demands that all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic provide full, safe, and unhindered access to the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission to conduct its work in Douma and any other relevant areas in accordance with its mandate;

6. Calls upon all Member States to cooperate fully with the UNIMI, including by providing relevant information regarding any use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic;

7. Requests the UNIMI to submit a first report to the Security Council and the OPCW Executive Council within ninety days of commencing its full operations, and regular reports every sixty days thereafter;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

